hello my name's David Haws and in my  previous presentation I explained to you  how crackling was invented and has  contributed substantially to these field  of structural biology and now I'd like  to give you an example of an actual  benefit that was derived from that which  is the HIV epidemic and some of the  drugs that were developed through  structural biology  once again we need to thank Max Perutz  who began the field of structural  biology and at the great vision that  three-dimensional structures not only  permit the identification of how the  molecules act in living organisms but in  fact how the structures can be  determined as well as how drugs can now  be designed using the process called  structure based drug design so now I'd  like to tell you a story of how I have  experienced science for the benefit of  humanity while I was at the Cold Spring  Harbor Laboratory I was told that my  cryocooler  experiments and publications probably  advanced structural biology by 10 or 20  years and at the time you know 10 or 20  years ago scientific discoveries really  usually not a big deal and it didn't  have any significance to me until while  I was preparing for a presentation this  last April at the Protein Data Bank in  Rutgers they asked me to talk about  exactly what I'm telling you  the invention of coral crystallography  and while I was doing that I discovered  the AIDS epidemic and the Lazarus effect  and for those of you who don't know what  the Lazarus effect is in medicine as  disinfectants when many people are dying  and then some event occurs or where  these vast numbers of people stopped  dying and that's exactly what happened  with the AIDS epidemic in 1996  this diagram here is from the United  States statistics from the CDC but let  me just tell you first about the  worldwide AIDS epidemic which began in  1980 there have been 76 million people  who have been infected with HIV virus  since 1980 of whom 39 million have died  and today worldwide there were 37  million people still infected  there was no cure for AIDS as you know  but though there is a therapeutic  treatment and that's what I'm going to  explain so now if I can talk about the  United States this is the death curve  and the infection curve the red ones the  infection occur for AIDS in the United  States so far since 1980 700,000  Americans have died from AIDS and as you  see the curve begins in 1918 continual  increases with hundreds of thousands of  individuals becoming infected with a  with HIV virus each year since the  average lifetime if you had caught HIV  and then during the 80s is typically 2  to 4 years your statistics would move  from the red curve to the blue curve  which is the death rate in about two  years and you see that the death rate is  continuous from 1980 to 1996 it's a  straight line basically which means that  none of the drugs and there were many  drugs that were tried produced any  effect on the death rate in 1984 the HIV  virus was identified as being the causal  agent of HIV and then in 1986 proteins  were isolated one of which is this  protein which is HIV protease and one of  the enzymes that makes HIV virus the  crystal structure was actually  determined the three-dimensional  structure in 1989 and we can thank Merck  laboratory in New Jersey for not only  solved  structure but they published the data to  the whole world in the protein data bank  in 1989 this gave the opportunity for  hundreds of other laboratories to work  on the HIV protease it turned out that  inhibitors were actually made and they  were made using structural biology using  cryocooler  and in 1996 the therapy was introduced  it was this cocktail of three three  drugs which the HIV protease inhibitor  was the most important and instead of  dying you would you would live a normal  life with in fact it was called a  manageable disease and because of that  the individuals did not transfer to the  death curve and you see this precipitous  fall in the death curve in 1996 the  death rate fell from 96 percent down to  below 20 percent so you can just imagine  between 1980 and 1996 if you had become  infected with HIV and you went to your  physician and he would tell you you had  a four percent chance of surviving but  in 1996 you would go in to go to your  doctor and he would say yes sir today  there's a therapy he will not be cured  of HIV but you will in fact become a  manageable disease and this dramatic  fall in the death rate in 1996 is called  the Lazarus effect tens if not hundreds  of thousands of Americans who would not  live and basically the HIV protease  inhibitors of drugs which were developed  were developed years and years faster  than they would have been if there had  not been structural biology and as it  turns out cryocooler which participated  in that so it makes me feel very good  that literally my research done 50 years  ago not ending up in dusty pages in a  journal  actually saving thousands of life that  really is science for the benefit of  humanity experiments that you do  publications that you make as a young  scientist in fact could contribute very  much to the future so I hope you've  become a little bit inspired with the  work that I have done and in fact it  expired me and maybe it'll inspire you  to continue thank you  you
 

